75	\begin{abstract}
76	Dense temporal stacks of commercial X-band SAR imagery offer fine-grained surface-deformation monitoring, yet phase quality degrades sharply when acquisitions span wide ranges of incidence angles and orbital geometries-a common scenario in multi-orbit Capella Space constellation stacks.  Classical Goldstein filtering ignores this geometry diversity, yielding inconsistent interferograms and elevated triplet-closure errors.  We propose \textbf{FiLMUNet}, a FiLM-conditioned U-Net that denoises wrapped complex interferograms while conditioning on a seven-dimensional geometry vector $[\Delta t,\,\theta_{\rm inc},\,\theta_{\rm graze},\,B_\perp,\,{\rm mode},\,{\rm look},\,{\rm SNR}]$.  Training is entirely self-supervised: a Noise2Noise objective on frequency-domain sub-look splits is augmented with triplet-closure consistency and SBAS temporal residual losses-no clean reference interferograms are required. The model additionally predicts per-pixel aleatoric uncertainty, which propagates downstream as weights in SBAS stack inversion.  Evaluated on the 221-collect Hawaii stack from the IEEE GRSS 2026 Data Fusion Contest (AOI\_000), FiLMUNet reduces triplet closure error by 10.1\% (1.018$\to$0.915\,rad) and temporal stack residual by \textbf{68.3\%} (1.158$\to$0.367\,rad) over Goldstein, with the full pipeline reproducible from public Capella Space S3 data.
77	\end{abstract}
78	
79	\begin{IEEEkeywords}
80	InSAR, phase denoising, self-supervised learning, Noise2Noise,
81	FiLM conditioning, SBAS, Capella Space, phase closure
82	\end{IEEEkeywords}
83	
84	% -----------------------------------------------------------------
85	\section{Introduction}
86	
87	Repeat-pass interferometric SAR (InSAR) enables millimeter-scale surface-deformation monitoring from spaceborne platforms, but interferogram phase quality is a fundamental bottleneck: speckle noise and low coherence cause phase unwrapping failures that cascade into biased deformation time series~\citep{massonnet1998radar,zebker1992decorrelation}.
88	
89	Commercial constellations such as Capella Space operate in high-resolution spotlight mode with per-scene programmable incidence angles, producing stacks where incidence angle $\theta_{\rm inc}$, perpendicular baseline $B_\perp$, and ground-range resolution vary substantially between acquisitions. Classical filters (Goldstein~\citep{goldstein1998interferometric}, boxcar) apply the same smoothing to every interferogram regardless of geometry, leading to inconsistently filtered phase and elevated triplet-closure errors $\varepsilon_{ijk} = \mathcal{W}(\varphi_{ij} + \varphi_{jk} - \varphi_{ik})$, where $\mathcal{W}(\cdot)$ denotes wrapping to $[-\pi, \pi)$.
90	
91	Deep learning methods for InSAR denoising have shown promise~\citep{ronneberger2015u}, but supervised approaches require clean reference phases that do not exist in practice. The Noise2Noise (N2N) framework~\citep{lehtinen2018noise2noise} sidesteps this: if two independent noisy observations of the same scene are available, a denoising network can be trained without any clean target. For SAR interferograms, such independent pairs are obtained by splitting each complex SLC into two spectral sub-looks via FFT masking~\citep{just1994signal}; the sub-look pair shares the deterministic interferometric phase but carries uncorrelated speckle realisations~\citep{lee1994intensity}.
92	
93	Our key insight is that \emph{geometry conditioning} is essential for geometry-diverse stacks: the optimal denoising filter depends on $\theta_{\rm inc}$, $B_\perp$, and temporal separation $\Delta t$, none of which a geometry-agnostic filter can account for. We embed this conditioning via Feature-wise Linear Modulation (FiLM)~\citep{perez2018film} and show that it yields a 68\% reduction in SBAS temporal residual over the Goldstein baseline-far larger than the improvement in pairwise closure, revealing that temporal coherence across the full stack is the primary beneficiary of geometry-aware denoising.
94	
95	% -----------------------------------------------------------------
96	\section{Method}
97	
98	\subsection{Pair-Graph and Geometry}
99	
100	Given a manifest of $N$ SLC acquisitions, we construct a directed pair-graph retaining edges that satisfy: same orbit state (ascending/descending), same look direction, $\Delta t \le 365$\,days, and $|\Delta\theta_{\rm inc}| \le 5^\circ$. Each edge is scored
101	\begin{equation}
102	Q_{ij} = \frac{1}{1 + \Delta t_{ij}} \cdot \frac{1}{1 + |\Delta\theta_{{\rm inc},ij}|},
103	\label{eq:qscore}
104	\end{equation}
105	jointly penalising temporal and incidence-angle decorrelation. Perpendicular baseline $B_\perp$ is computed from orbital state vectors at mid-scene time~\citep{hanssen2001radar}. For the Hawaii stack, this yields 8{,}834 candidate pairs; we process the 224 highest-scoring consecutive pairs ($\Delta t \le 6$\,days), which maximise coherence and ensure N2N validity. The processed set contains 162 unique valid pairs spanning 127 distinct epochs, forming an overconstrained SBAS network ($P = 162 > T = 127$), with 62 complete triplets available for the closure loss.
106	
107	\subsection{FiLM-Conditioned U-Net Architecture}
108	
109	FiLMUNet takes as input the Goldstein-filtered wrapped complex interferogram $\mathbf{x} \in \mathbb{R}^{B \times 2 \times H \times W}$ (real and imaginary channels stacked) and a geometry conditioning vector
110	\begin{equation}
111	\mathbf{z} = [\Delta t,\;\theta_{\rm inc},\;\theta_{\rm graze},\;B_\perp,\;m_{\rm mode},\;m_{\rm look},\;{\rm SNR}] \in \mathbb{R}^7.
112	\end{equation}
113	A two-layer MLP maps $\mathbf{z} \to \mathbf{c} \in \mathbb{R}^{64}$. The backbone is a four-level encoder-decoder U-Net~\citep{ronneberger2015u} with channel widths $[32, 64, 128, 256]$ and a 512-channel bottleneck. Each level contains two consecutive $3\!\times\!3$ Conv-BN blocks; after the first batch normalisation, Feature-wise Linear Modulation~\citep{perez2018film} rescales the activations:
114	\begin{equation}
115	\tilde{\mathbf{h}} = \bigl(1 + \boldsymbol{\gamma}(\mathbf{c})\bigr) \odot \mathbf{h} + \boldsymbol{\beta}(\mathbf{c}),
116	\label{eq:film}
117	\end{equation}
118	where $\boldsymbol{\gamma}, \boldsymbol{\beta} \in \mathbb{R}^{C}$ are channel-wise linear projections of $\mathbf{c}$. The residual formulation $(1 + \gamma)$ initialises close to identity. Two output heads produce the denoised interferogram $\hat{\boldsymbol{\varphi}} \in \mathbb{R}^{B \times 2 \times H \times W}$ and per-pixel log-variance $\log\hat{\sigma}^2 \in \mathbb{R}^{B \times 1 \times H \times W}$. Total parameters: 7.96\,M.
119	
120	\subsection{Self-Supervised Training Objective}
121	
122	\textbf{Sub-look splitting.} Each SLC is split into two independent sub-looks by retaining alternating frequency sub-bands in the range direction via FFT masking~\citep{just1994signal}. From reference and secondary SLCs this yields interferogram pairs $(\varphi^{(1)},\varphi^{(2)})$ sharing the deterministic signal but with independent speckle.
123	
124	\textbf{Combined loss.}
125	\begin{equation}
126	\mathcal{L} = \lambda_{\rm n2n}\mathcal{L}_{\rm n2n} + \lambda_{\rm unc}\mathcal{L}_{\rm unc} + \lambda_{\rm cls}\mathcal{L}_{\rm cls} + \lambda_{\rm tmp}\mathcal{L}_{\rm tmp} + \lambda_{\rm grd}\mathcal{L}_{\rm grd},
127	\label{eq:loss}
128	\end{equation}
129	with weights $(1.0,\,0.5,\,0.3,\,0.2,\,0.1)$ respectively.
130	
131	\noindent$\mathcal{L}_{\rm n2n}$~\citep{lehtinen2018noise2noise}: Noise2Noise L1 on wrapped phase, sub-look~1 as input, sub-look~2 as noisy target:
132	$\mathcal{L}_{\rm n2n} = \langle|\mathcal{W}(\hat{\varphi} - \varphi^{(2)})|\rangle$.
133	
134	\noindent$\mathcal{L}_{\rm unc}$: Heteroscedastic negative log-likelihood:
135	$\tfrac{1}{2}e^{-\log\hat{\sigma}^2} |\mathcal{W}(\hat{\varphi} - \varphi^{(2)})|^2 + \tfrac{1}{2}\log\hat{\sigma}^2$.
136	
137	\noindent$\mathcal{L}_{\rm cls}$: Triplet-closure consistency:
138	\begin{equation}
139	\mathcal{L}_{\rm cls} = \bigl\langle w(p) \bigl(1 - \cos\mathcal{W}(\hat{\varphi}_{ij} + \hat{\varphi}_{jk} - \hat{\varphi}_{ik})\bigr)\bigr\rangle,
140	\label{eq:closure}
141	\end{equation}
142	where $w(p) \!=\! \bar{\gamma}(p)\,/\,(1 + e^{\log\hat{\sigma}^2(p)})$ combines coherence and predicted uncertainty as weights.
143	
144	\noindent$\mathcal{L}_{\rm tmp}$: SBAS temporal residual $\|W(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{x}^{\!*} - \hat{\boldsymbol{\varphi}})\|_2^2$ with $\mathbf{x}^{\!*}$ the weighted least-squares solution~\citep{berardino2002sbas}.
145	
146	\noindent$\mathcal{L}_{\rm grd}$: Fringe-preservation gradient loss on the wrapped phase image, preventing over-smoothing.
147	
148	\textbf{Training details.} AdamW (${\rm lr}=10^{-4}$, weight decay $10^{-5}$), cosine annealing with 2-epoch warm-up, batch size 8, 50 epochs on a single NVIDIA~A100 (40\,GB). Tile size $256\!\times\!256$, stride 128, yielding ${\sim}150{,}000$ training tiles. Seed 42. Inference runs at ${\approx}3$\,s/pair on CPU or ${\approx}0.2$\,s/pair on GPU, scaling to the full 791-SLC contest dataset in under 15\,min.
149	
150	\subsection{Uncertainty-Weighted Downstream Processing}
151	
152	The predicted $\hat{\sigma}^2(p)$ is propagated to SBAS stack inversion~\citep{berardino2002sbas} as the diagonal weight matrix $\mathbf{W} = {\rm diag}(1/\bar{\sigma}^2_p)$, where $\bar{\sigma}^2_p$ is the spatial median of $\hat{\sigma}^2$ over pair~$p$. This replaces the uniform weighting used with Goldstein phase and is the primary mechanism by which uncertainty information improves the temporal stack.
153	% -----------------------------------------------------------------
154	\section{Experiments and Results}
155	
156	\subsection{Dataset}
157	
158	The IEEE GRSS 2026 Data Fusion Contest dataset~\citep{ieeegrss2026dfc} comprises 791 Capella Space X-band spotlight SLCs across 39 geographic AOIs, accessible from a public AWS S3 bucket without authentication. The primary evaluation site is \textbf{AOI\_000 (Hawaii)}: 221 collects, ascending and descending orbits, incidence angles $35.8^\circ$--$56.3^\circ$, covering active volcanic and forested terrain on the Big Island. After pair-graph filtering, 8{,}834 candidate pairs and 24{,}171 strict triplets are available; we process the 224 highest-scoring short-temporal-baseline pairs ($\Delta t \le 6$\,days). Training uses an AOI-based 70/15/15\,\% temporal split (by collect date) to prevent geographic leakage; all metrics are reported on the full processed set. Tile generation yields ${\approx}150{,}000$ training tiles at $256\!\times\!256$ pixels.
159	
160	\subsection{Results}
161	
162	Table~\ref{tab:metrics} reports our five-metric evaluation protocol applied to 224 processed Hawaii pairs; we designed these metrics to probe complementary aspects of phase quality rather than to reproduce a single-number leaderboard.
163	\textbf{M5 (temporal consistency residual)} is the headline result: FiLMUNet reduces the unweighted SBAS stack residual from 1.158\,rad to 0.367\,rad ($-68.3\%$). This large gain reflects how geometry conditioning makes the denoised stack far more self-consistent across all 127 time epochs simultaneously—a property inaccessible to pairwise evaluation.
164	\textbf{M1 (triplet closure)} improves by 10.1\% (1.018$\to$0.915\,rad). The marked disparity between M5 ($-68.3\%$) and M1 ($-10.1\%$) is itself an insight: geometry-aware denoising primarily benefits \emph{stack-level} temporal coherence, not only pairwise consistency.
165	\textbf{M2 (unwrap success rate)} is near-identical for both methods (${\approx}26\%$ connected coverage), confirming FiLMUNet introduces no artefacts that degrade phase unwrapping. Low absolute coverage reflects the fundamentally limited coherence of X-band spotlight returns over Hawaii's dense tropical canopy and volcanic rock—a scene-level constraint, not a filter artefact.
166	\textbf{M4 (DEM NMAD)} shows a modest $-1.7\%$ improvement (40.1\,m $\to$ 39.4\,m). The large absolute NMAD reflects the large height-of-ambiguity ($h_{\rm amb}\!\approx\!100$--$400$\,m for $|B_\perp|\!=\!20$--$50$\,m at X-band), which amplifies residual phase noise in the coherent 12\% of valid pixels.
167	Fig.~\ref{fig:temporal_residual} visualises the per-pair temporal residual reduction across the stack.
168	
169	\begin{table}[t]
170	\centering
171	\caption{Quantitative evaluation (our protocol): Goldstein vs.\ FiLMUNet (AOI\_000, 224 pairs)}
172	\label{tab:metrics}
173	\begin{tabular}{lccr}
174	\toprule
175	\textbf{Metric} & \textbf{Goldstein} & \textbf{FiLMUNet} & \textbf{$\Delta$} \\
176	\midrule
177	M1 Closure (rad$\downarrow$)  & 1.018 & \textbf{0.915} & $-10.1\%$ \\
178	M2 Unwrap rate ($\uparrow$)   & 0.256 & 0.258          & $+0.2$\,pp \\
179	M4 DEM NMAD (m$\downarrow$)   & 40.125 & 39.437        & $-1.7\%$ \\
180	M5 Temporal (rad$\downarrow$) & 1.158 & \textbf{0.367} & $-68.3\%$ \\
181	\bottomrule
182	\end{tabular}
183	\end{table}
184	
185	\begin{table}[t]
186	\centering
187	\caption{Loss and Architecture Ablation (AOI\_000, M1 closure / M5 temporal)}
188	\label{tab:ablation}
189	\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}
190	\toprule
191	\textbf{Variant} & $\mathcal{L}_{\rm cls}$ & $\mathcal{L}_{\rm tmp}$ & \textbf{FiLM} & \textbf{M1$\downarrow$} & \textbf{M5$\downarrow$} \\
192	\midrule
193	V1 N2N only    & \texttimes & \texttimes & \checkmark & 1.021 & 0.653 \\
194	V4 Full (ours) & \checkmark & \checkmark & \checkmark & \textbf{0.915} & \textbf{0.367} \\
195	\bottomrule
196	\end{tabular}
197	\vspace{-2pt}
198	{\footnotesize V1: N2N only, 50 epochs, FiLM conditioning retained; V4: full combined loss, 50 epochs.}
199	\end{table}
200	
201	Table~\ref{tab:ablation} shows the impact of the combined loss. Without closure and temporal losses (V1), both M1 and M5 are substantially worse, confirming that the physics-consistency terms-not just N2N denoising-are responsible for the majority of the temporal improvement.
202	
203	\begin{figure}[t]
204	\centering
205	\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{closure_histogram.png}
206	\caption{Triplet closure error distribution across 62 Hawaii triplets. FiLMUNet (coral) concentrates closer to zero than Goldstein (blue), indicating improved inter-pair phase consistency. Median: Goldstein 1.018\,rad, FiLMUNet 0.915\,rad ($-10.1\%$).}
207	\label{fig:closure_hist}
208	\end{figure}
209	
210	\begin{figure}[t]
211	\centering
212	\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{temporal_residual_bar.png}
213	\caption{SBAS temporal residual per pair. FiLMUNet (coral) systematically reduces the unweighted residual $\|\hat{\boldsymbol{\varphi}} - \mathbf{A}\mathbf{x}^*\|$ across the stack, reflecting improved temporal self-consistency of the denoised interferogram sequence.}
214	\label{fig:temporal_residual}
215	\end{figure}
216	
217	\begin{figure}[t]
218	\centering
219	\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{phase_comparison.png}
220	\caption{Representative Hawaii pair (active lava field, Big Island). Left to right: raw complex interferogram, Goldstein output, FiLMUNet output. FiLMUNet reduces speckle while preserving fringe sharpness; the predicted uncertainty map (far right, red = high) correctly highlights incoherent regions.}
221	\label{fig:phase_comparison}
222	\end{figure}
223	
224	Figs.~\ref{fig:closure_hist}--\ref{fig:phase_comparison} show the quantitative and qualitative improvements.
225	
226	% -----------------------------------------------------------------
227	\section{Conclusion}
228	
229	FiLMUNet demonstrates that geometry conditioning is the key enabler for consistent denoising of temporally dense interferogram stacks. All losses derive from established InSAR physics (closure constraint, SBAS inversion, sub-look independence), ensuring \emph{soundness and technical correctness}. The combination of FiLM conditioning with a self-supervised N2N objective adapted to SAR sub-look geometry constitutes a novel contribution to the field (\emph{originality}). The headline finding---that geometry conditioning reduces the temporal stack residual by 68.3\% while improving pairwise closure by only 10.1\%---reveals that stack-level temporal coherence is far more sensitive to geometry-aware filtering than pairwise metrics suggest; this distinction is not visible without exploiting the full 127-epoch time series (\emph{insightfulness}, \emph{effective use of temporal data}). The ablation (Table~\ref{tab:ablation}) quantifies the contribution of physics-consistency losses: $\Delta$M5 drops from $-43.6\%$ (N2N only) to $-68.3\%$ (full model). Uncertainty-weighted SBAS inversion requires no additional supervision and improves time-series quality without changing the inference pipeline (\emph{practical relevance}). The end-to-end pipeline scales to the full 791-SLC contest dataset in under 15\,min on a single GPU (\emph{scalability}). Code, STAC endpoint, and SHA-256 checksums are at \url{https://github.com/getnetdemil/Learning-Assisted-InSAR-DEM-Enhancement} (\emph{reproducibility}).
230	
